Subject: [M156] blink: Fix ImageCaptureFrameGrabber UAF in OnVideoFrame

Original change's description:
> blink: Fix ImageCaptureFrameGrabber UAF in OnVideoFrame
>
> ImageCapture is an Oilpan GarbageCollected object that owns a
> non-Oilpan ImageCaptureFrameGrabber helper [1], which is constructed,
> used, and destroyed exclusively on the main thread. In grabFrame() [2],
> ImageCaptureFrameGrabber::GrabFrame() connects a SingleShotFrameHandler
> to the media stream track [3]. When a frame arrives on the IO thread,
> SingleShotFrameHandler::OnVideoFrameOnIOThread() posts
> ImageCaptureFrameGrabber::OnVideoFrame() back to the main thread task
> runner bound with a WeakPtr<ImageCaptureFrameGrabber> [4] (so the
> WeakPtr is only ever dereferenced and invalidated on the main thread).
> Because nothing holds a Persistent<ImageCapture> while grabFrame() is
> pending, ImageCapture becomes unreachable in Oilpan if JavaScript drops
> its reference to the ImageCapture wrapper.
>
> This leads to a same-thread re-entrant UAF on the main thread:
> 1. ImageCaptureFrameGrabber::OnVideoFrame() starts on the main thread
>    [5] before Oilpan has swept the dead ImageCapture. base::WeakPtr
>    only checks validity at callback entry; it does not keep
>    ImageCaptureFrameGrabber alive while OnVideoFrame() is on the stack.
> 2. OnVideoFrame() calls GetRasterContextProvider() [5] ->
>    SharedGpuContext::ContextProviderWrapper() ->
>    SharedGpuContext::CreateContextProviderIfNeeded() [6] ->
>    RendererBlinkPlatformImpl::
>    CreateSharedOffscreenGraphicsContext3DProvider() ->
>    viz::Gpu::EstablishGpuChannelSync() [7] on the main thread.
> 3. EstablishGpuChannelSync() calls EstablishRequest::FinishOnMain() ->
>    Gpu::OnEstablishedGpuChannel() -> Gpu::RunEstablishCallbacks() [8],
>    which synchronously runs any queued EstablishGpuChannel callbacks
>    inline on the main thread before returning.
> 4. A queued callback (e.g. from an earlier navigator.gpu.requestAdapter
>    call [9]) runs inline on the main thread and resolves/rejects its
>    promise, which allocates on the Oilpan heap and triggers Oilpan lazy
>    sweeping synchronously on the main thread.
> 5. Lazy sweeping runs ~ImageCapture() inline on the main thread,
>    deleting frame_grabber_ while OnVideoFrame() is still on the stack.
>    When GetRasterContextProvider() returns to OnVideoFrame(), "this" is
>    dangling [5].
>
> We cannot simply bind OnVideoFrame() with WrapPersistent like
> getPhotoCapabilities, getPhotoSettings, and takePhoto do:
> - ImageCaptureFrameGrabber is not an Oilpan GarbageCollected class (it
>   subclasses MediaStreamVideoSink and relies on ~MediaStreamVideoSink()
>   to disconnect from the track), so WrapPersistent cannot be used on it.
> - Routing OnVideoFrame() through ImageCapture with a CrossThreadHandle
>   would break cancellation on ContextDestroyed(): whereas
>   HeapMojoRemote automatically drops pending callbacks on
>   ContextDestroyed(), OnVideoFrame() is posted from the IO thread via
>   PostCrossThreadTask to a raw SingleThreadTaskRunner and relies on
>   ImageCapture::ContextDestroyed() resetting frame_grabber_ (and
>   invalidating its WeakPtr) to cancel any pending or already-posted
>   frame grab.
>
> Instead, make ImageCaptureFrameGrabber RefCounted (single-threaded on
> the main thread, shared only between ImageCapture and an actively
> executing OnVideoFrame() call) and hold a local
> scoped_refptr<ImageCaptureFrameGrabber> keep_alive on the stack across
> OnVideoFrame().
>
> Note: The bug report's suggested patch also included a test for
> ImageCapture::ContextDestroyed() running synchronously during
> OnVideoFrame(), but auditing the EstablishGpuChannel() callers shows
> that none of them can synchronously run JavaScript or detach a frame
> (promise resolution uses V8DoNotRunMicrotasksScope), so synchronous
> execution context destruction during OnVideoFrame() is not possible.
>
